WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:08.500
Well the basic premise of the project was
that I was hoping to find out as much about

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.770
myself as I could, and specifically my DNA,
without any of the sort of safeguards that

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:17.220
you would expect that you would have to go
through in order to get that sort of information.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.840
I didn't go through a doctor, I didn't register
with a genetic counsellor, there was nobody

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.130
getting me ready for what the results of my
tests would be. My intention was to be out

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:29.400
there in kind of the wilds of the internet,
with just me and my credit card. These companies

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:35.470
had no idea who I was, I was just buying data.
Companies were selling DNA tests offering

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:39.160
you a variety of different stories about your
life. There are DNA tests which were sold

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:45.300
on fear, so they were offers to package your
DNA, store your DNA, allow you to identify

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:49.930
children who'd gone missing or make it easier
to identify you if you were killed. DNA tests

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:53.310
which offered to tell you something fundamental
about your history, so they would tell you

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:57.440
where your family came from, where your DNA
came from, and the great myths of your past.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.570
Tests which offered to predict the future
for you; they would tell you when you will

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:04.610
die, what illnesses you would get. And finally
there were the testers who were just offering

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:08.390
you fun, essentially. They would make you
a necklace or a T-shirt or a stained-glass

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:13.511
window or a tie, based on some fairly unimportant
bits of your DNA sequence. Most of the tests

00:01:13.511 --> 00:01:18.049
required that you take a cheek cell swab.
So basically you get an over-sized Q-tip,

00:01:18.049 --> 00:01:21.330
and rub it quite hard on the inside of each
cheek and underneath your tongue. You have

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:27.140
to be quite harsh when you do it; it hurts.
Your tongue and your cheeks will smart afterwards

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:34.870
if you do it properly.

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:44.140
So I'm holding in my hands the result of an
ancestry test which I had done, and this is

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:48.070
a autosomal DNA test, so they tested all my
DNA, to find out where it had come from in

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:56.590
the world and what my deep ancestral roots
are. According to this, I'm 92 percent European,

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:04.790
6 percent East Asian, 2 percent Sub-Saharan
African and 0 percent Native American. I have

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:09.729
to admit I was hoping for something a little
bit more interesting. I have here the results

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:15.010
of another one of my tests. I've had something
called the ACTN3 gene tested. Elite sportsmen

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:19.950
tend to have one particular form, and the
rest of us have a different form entirely.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:26.349
So it turns out I'm not going to be a great
footballer when I grow up. But on the other

00:02:26.349 --> 00:02:29.150
hand, if there's ever anything I need to do
for a very long time, I have a lot of stamina.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:34.900
  I have a very low risk of glaucoma thankfully.
I do however have a very high risk of age-related

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:40.410
macular degeneration. So there's a good chance
that my eyes are going to pack in when I get

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:44.480
older, which is not the best news I've had
all day, The DNA test results I've just had

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.260
through are from a company looking at a variety
of different genes for me, and one of the

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:50.860
genes they're telling me about is called APOE,
and they're very happy to tell me all about

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.940
m APOE results because I've got two versions
of this gene, both of which are the same,

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:59.680
and they're both called APOE3. It would tend
to mean that I have a lower cholesterol level.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:04.599
The other good thing, that they haven't mentioned,
is that having two copies of APOE3 means that

00:03:04.599 --> 00:03:10.760
I don't have the high-risk APOE4 version which
would mean that I'd be more likely to develop

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:15.050
Alzheimer's later in life. The other great
things about this is that if I don't have

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:18.150
any copies of it, that means neither of my
parents have enough copies to have a high-risk

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:19.150
allele, either.  So here I've got the second
set of results. This company have tested my

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:20.150
APOE gene exactly as the other one did. Immediately
I've noticed something quite strange. This

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:21.150
company is saying that I'm APOE3/E4, so they
say I have two different versions. So I'm

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.150
slightly confused and not quite sure what
result I should be taking from it.  So I'm

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:24.050
now reviewing another test. They've tested
four genes that are involved in venous thrombosis.

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:28.860
There is something slightly worrying in here.
This is the first time that I've had the words

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:35.490
'potentially high' in bold, in capital letters,
in the section about my risk level. It turns

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:39.810
out that I have genetic variations that are
believed to increase my risk over the general

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:47.490
population by a factor of about fifty. Thankfully,
I'm not old, I'm not pregnant, I'm not using

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:53.099
an oral contraceptive, I'm not obese, I don't
have diabetes, I've not had any recent major

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:58.569
surgery. So all of those factors I'm alright
on, but the genetic factors I'm not doing

00:03:58.569 --> 00:04:02.659
very well on at all.  A lot of these results,
if I hadn't been a geneticist in the past,

00:04:02.659 --> 00:04:06.650
I would have opened them and probably been
quite scared.  Why on earth would anyone

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:10.610
want to know all these things about themselves?
I think I've found out quite a lot of little

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:14.900
titbits about myself but nothing that's particularly
going to change the way that I live my life.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:18.699
I'm not going to grow up to be a great sportsman,
but then I'm thirty-one and working in science,

00:04:18.699 --> 00:04:22.439
I was never going to be a great sportsman.
I might get fat when I get old, my eyes might

00:04:22.439 --> 00:04:27.610
not work, I might get heart disease, I think
that's true of absolutely everybody.  I've

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:30.740
learnt some fairly meaningless things about
myself. Supposedly I'm related to a woman

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:36.610
who lived in Italy 15,000 years ago and supposedly
I have the most European DNA of anyone you're

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.809
likely to meet, but I don't think either of
those is going to change the way that I live

00:04:39.809 --> 00:04:39.859
my life.

